# Untitled

Metal-organic frameworks-assisted nonenzymatic cascade amplification multiplexed strategy for sensing acute myocardial infarction related microRNAs
a School of Pharmacy, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, Jiangsu, 211166, PR China b Key Laboratory of Cardiovascular & Cerebrovascular Medicine, School of Pharmacy, Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, Jiangsu, 211166, PR China c Department of Laboratory Medicine, The First Affiliated Hospital of Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, Jiangsu, 211166, PR China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
Keywords: Acute myocardial infarction Multiple microRNA detection Signal amplification Simultaneous detection Metal-organic frameworks
## A B S T R A C T
Amplification strategies for multiple microRNAs (miRNAs) detection are pivotal for acute myocardial infarction (AMI). Herein, we rationally developed a metal-organic frameworks-assisted nonenzymatic cascade amplifica­ tion strategy for simultaneous quantification of three AMI-related miRNAs (miR-21, miR-499 and miR-133a). The fluorescence of the elaborately designed DNA molecular beacons with the respective modification of FAM, TAMRA and Cy5 in the terminal was quenched by a metal-organic framework named Fe-MIL-88. When targets miRNA appeared, they hybridized with the corresponding DNA molecular beacons, and the catalyzed hairpin assembly (CHA) reaction would be triggered, producing “Y” shaped three-branched duplex nanostructure with the targets released, and initiating subsequent another cycle. The “Y” shaped nanostructures could not be adsorbed onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88 due to the weaker affinity between Fe-MIL-88 and “Y” shaped nano­ structures. Therefore, the fluorescence of “Y” shaped nanostructures could not be quenched by Fe-MIL-88. In this way, three AMI-related miRNAs were simultaneously detected in the respective ranges of 0.05–30 nM, 0.08–30 nM and $0.1{-}20~\mathrm{nM}$ with respective limits of detection down to 13, 25 and $40\ \mathsf{p M}$ . Furthermore, the method was successfully employed to determine three AMI-related miRNAs in human serum. The strategy offered great opportunity for ultrasensitive detecting multiple AMI-related miRNAs and substantially improving the accuracy of clinical early AMI diagnosis.
## 1. Introduction
As a class of the most common coronary syndromes, acute myocar­ dial infarction (AMI) with stenosis of vascular lumen, acute and persistent hypoxic-ischemic myocardial ischemia and injuries can result in severe disabilities and ultrahigh mortality rates (Thygesen et al., 2012; Reed et al., 2016). At present, high-sensitive cardiac troponin (hs-cTnT) is recognized as gold standard for diagnosing AMI (Kavsak et al., 2011). However, elevated plasma hs-cTnT also appears in chronic stable coronary artery disease (Shiozaki et al., 2017) and end-stage renal disease (Menon et al., 2009). Additionally, cTnT exhibits significant raised levels only around 6 h with newest high sensitive immunoassays (Chen et al., 2013). In this regard, discovering better potential biomarkers is particular important for rapid and accurate diagnosing the early stage of AMI.
MicroRNA (miRNA), a short endogenous noncoding RNA, plays vital roles in biological process through regulating the expression of gene at the post-transcriptional levels (Liu et al., 2018, 2019; Li et al., 2021). Growing evidences have proved that the myocardial tissues express miRNAs specifically, such as miRNA-21 (miR-21), miRNA-499 (miR-499), and miRNA-133a (miR-133a) (Mao et al., 2019). Notably, miR-21 is able to enhance the function of stem and progenitor cells in AMI and results in AMI fibrogenesis and cellular hypertrophy (Thum et al., 2008). While miR-499, a potential biomarker for reperfusion injury, is physiologically associated with cardiac differentiation and ischemia/reperfusion injury (Wang et al., 2011). Especially, miR-133a plays crucial roles in mediating cardiac processes including conduc­ tance, automaticity, hypertrophy and cardiac remodeling (Xiao et al., 2019). Moreover, the expression level of relevant miRNAs in the plasma and serum is found promptly upregulated to a significant extent in pa­ tients’ bloodstream even in 1 h following an AMI event (Creemers et al.,
2012). In this sense, these miRNAs can be considered as potential bio­ markers for prognosing early AMI quickly with high sensitivity and specificity over antibody-based analogues. Currently, various methods have been reported for AMI-related miRNAs including northern blot, real-time polymerase chain reaction, surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy, microarrays and so on (Jia et al., 2010). However, most of these techniques are time-consuming and lack of sensitivity due to the unique characteristics of miRNAs in biological samples including small size, low abundance and strong homology (Zhou et al., 2019). On the other hand, traditional approaches on the basis of single AMI-related miRNA determinations exclusively may yield false positive or false negative results inevitably. To overcome these obstacles, techniques combined effective nucleic acid signal amplification with multiplexing capability are needed urgently.
In order to further improve the sensitivity for simultaneous quanti­ fication, nanomaterials have been introduced (Li et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2015). In particular, MOFs, a distinctive new class of nano-porous organic-inorganic materials, possess fascinating structures and intriguing properties including extraordinarily high surface and flexible porosity, tailorable molecular properties, and inherent good thermal stability (Yang et al., 2020; Deng et al., 2019). Inspiringly, several MOFs including Cu-MOF and Fe-MOF have been designed for DNA sensing (Yang et al., 2015; Tian et al., 2015). These MOFs exhibit extraordinary fluorescence quenching ability toward various dyes-labeled single-­ stranded DNA (ssDNA) as well as preferential binding ability to ssDNA over double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) owing to the stronger adsorption ability of MOFs toward ssDNA through van der Waals force and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction. Unfortunately, single step detection based on MOFs and dyes-labeled DNA has low readout signal and limits its sensitivity due to one-step hybridization. It is encouraging that the detection signal can be amplified effectively with the assistance of catalytic hairpin as­ sembly (CHA)-assisted target recycling (Duan et al., 2021; Lv et al., 2020). To the best of our knowledge, MOFs-based fluorometric assay for multiplexed microRNA analysis with extraordinary sensitivity and specificity by combining CHA amplification techniques remains largely unexplored.
Herein, a novel multiplexed isothermal detection platform for AMIrelated miRNAs was presented by integrating the advantages of MOF and CHA. Nine hairpin DNAs were modified with fluorescent dyes at the sticky end (FAM, TAMRA and Cy5 corresponded to miR-21, miR-499 and miR-133a, respectively). Specifically, hairpin DNAs were adsorbed on the surface of Fe-MIL-88 with their fluorescence quenched via fluo­ rescence resonance energy transfer effect (FRET). However, when target miRNAs were present, the hybridization between miRNAs and hairpin DNAs was activated, causing the formation of “Y” shaped nano­ structures, target miRNAs were then free again and recycled to the next cycle producing more “Y” shaped nanostructures, achieving signal amplification. Owing to the weaker affinity between the “Y” shaped nanostructures and Fe-MIL-88, the “Y” shaped nanostructures could not be adsorbed onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88 and the fluorescence was still retained. Finally, the simultaneous quantification of multiple AMIrelated miRNAs was realized. Our results demonstrated that the AMIrelated miRNAs in human serums can be accurately determined by the proposed approach.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Simultaneous quantification of multiple AMI-related miRNAs
$45~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of a mixture containing the prepared hairpin probe H1–H9 (each $200~\mathrm{nM})$ was incubated with $45~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of various concentrations of miR-21, miR-499 and miR-133a in Tris-HCl buffer ( $20~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris, $10\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ , pH 8.0) at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Afterwards, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Fe-MIL-88 $400\upmu\mathrm{g/}$ mL) was added and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The fluorescence spectra were measured at the excitation wavelength of $488\mathrm{nm}$ , $552\mathrm{nm}$ and $635\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and at the excitation of voltages of 650 V, 650 V and $850\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. All mentioned concentrations were the final concentrations.
## 2.2. Simultaneous quantification of multiple AMI-related miRNAs in human serum
Human serum samples were obtained from the First Affiliated Hospital-Nanjing Medical University (Jiangsu, China). After the samples were diluted 50-fold, the experimental process was performed as described above.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Principle of the proposed strategy
For the convenience of explanation, miR-21 was chosen as the example to illustrate the magnification strategy. Fig. 1A showed the situation of miR-21 detection without CHA. When hairpin H1 was adsorbed onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88 via van der Waals force and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions between overhanging part of H1 and Fe-MIL-88, the fluorescence of FAM modified at the sticky end of H1 was quenched efficiently due to FRET. When miR-21 was added, it hybrid­ ized with H1 to form dsDNA (H1/miR-21). A fluorescence recovery could be observed because it is difficult for dsDNA to be adsorbed onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88. However, the intensity of the fluorescence was faint since one miR-21 molecule just hybridized with one H1 to form one dsDNA, which possessed only one FAM fluorophore.
In order to enhance the fluorescence signal output, CHA was intro­ duced (Fig. 1B). We elaborately designed hairpin DNA probes H1, H2 and H3 with FAM at the sticky end on the basis of miR-21 sequence. The overhanging and stem part of H1 were partially complementary to target miR-21. The hairpin structure prevented toehold-mediated strand displacement in the absence of target due to the seal of recognition site (Fig. S1). After hairpin probes were adsorbed closely onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88, the fluorescence of FAM was quenched via FRET. However, upon the introduction of target miR-21, miR-21 hybridized with the overhanging part of H1, opening up the hairpin structure of H1 and sequentially producing a sticky end partly complementary to H2. Accompanied by opening up H2, the exposed sticky end of H2 activated the hybridization between H2 and H3. Ultimately, miR-21 was released due to a more stable state between H3 and H1 and simultaneously a “Y” shaped nanostructure with three FAM fluorophores was formed. After­ wards, the released miR-21 triggered a new round of hybridization repeatedly. In this case, prominent fluorescence signals could be ob­ tained because “Y” shaped nanostructure could not be adsorbed onto the surface of Fe-MIL-88 as well as one miR-21 molecule could be amplified effectively via producing several “Y” shaped nanostructures with 3n of FAM fluorophores, where n refers to the numbers of “Y” shaped nano­ structure. Notably, CHA strategy resulted in a remarkable enhance in fluorescence signals compared with the approach without amplification.
## 3.2. Characterization of Fe-MIL-88
The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) images of Fe-MIL-88 (Fig. 2A and Fig. 2B) clearly indicated the uniform octahedron morphology with diameters of approximately $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The successful formation of Fe-MIL-88 was confirmed by X-ray photoelectron spectra (Fig. S4A and Fig. S5), Fourier transform infrared spectrum (Fig. S4B) and X-ray diffraction pattern (Fig. S4C). A broad band is shown in UV–vis absorption spectrum (Fig. S4D), indicating that Fe-MIL-88 is a potential broad-spectrum fluorescence quencher. In particular, the fluorescence quenching effi­ ciency of Fe-MIL-88 was better than graphite oxide sheets and much better than $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanosheets and cobalt oxyhydroxide nanoflakes owing to the strong adsorption ability of Fe-MIL-88 toward hairpins with short overhanging ssDNA (around 10 nucleotide bases) (Fig. S4E). Importantly, the ability of discriminating ssDNA from dsDNA of Fe-MIL88 is stronger than GO in the range of $0{-}100~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ , while is inferior to GO in the range of $100{-}200~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ (Fig. S4F).
## 3.3. Proof-of-concept test
The feasibility was further verified by measuring the fluorescence of different dyes with Fe-MIL-88. Obvious decreased fluorescent signals of FAM, TAMRA and Cy5 were detected with increased concentrations of Fe-MIL-88 respectively, indicating that Fe-MIL-88 was a fantastic quenching nano-material (Fig. 2F, G and H). Moreover, Fe-MIL-88 possessed predominant discriminating ability between hairpin DNA and "Y" shaped nanostructure (Fig. S6).
The absorption spectrum of Fe-MIL-88 was well overlapped with the fluorescence emission spectra of FAM, TAMRA and Cy5 (Fig. S7A). Meanwhile, H1–H9 possessed opposite potentials compared with FeMIL-88 (Fig. S7B). Additionally, the fluorescence lifetime of the mixture of H1, H2 and H3 (or H4, H5 and H6 or H7, H8 and H9) was decreased after the addition of Fe-MIL-88, demonstrating FRET effect between hairpin probes and Fe-MIL-88 (Fig. S7C, S7D and S7E).
## 3.4. Analytical performance for single miRNA detection
Under optimal conditions (Fig. S8), the approach possessed a wider detection range of $0.01{-}20~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-21, $0.05\mathrm{-}12~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-499, and $0.008{-}20~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-133a (Fig. S9) while the approach without CHA were $2{\-}500\ \mathrm{nM}$ for miR-21, 3–400 nM for miR-499, and $2{-}300\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-133a (Fig. S10). The limits of detection (LODs) of CHA approach for miR-21, miR-499, and miR-133a were 1, 12, and $0.9\ \mathrm{pM}$ , respec­ tively, which were approximately 3 orders of magnitude lower than those of the approach without CHA (0.8 nM, 1.4 nM, 0.7 nM) according to 3σ rule (Fig. S13 A-F). Comparing the analytical performance of the two methods, the sensitivity of CHA system was tremendously enhanced than that of the method with single-step strand displacement. Mean­ while, considering the excellent ability of distinguishing three bio­ markers (Fig. S11A) as well as prominent specificity and cross-reactivity (Fig. S11B), our method demonstrated great potentials with regard to applications in further multiplex determination.
## 3.5. Analytical performance of the multiple detection platform
Under optimal conditions (Fig. S12), excellent linear relationships were obtained to be $0.05{-}30~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-21, $0.08{-}30~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-499 and $0.1{-}20~\mathrm{nM}$ for miR-133a (Fig. 3). The LODs for miR-21, miR-499, and miR-133a were 13, 25 and $40\ \mathrm{pM}$ , respectively, which were higher than those of single detection platform (Fig. S13 G-I). The reason is that in single detection platform, each target possessed its optimal Fe-MIL88, respectively, while in multiple detection platform, three targets shared Fe-MIL-88 with the same concentration which was not optimal for all targets. Additionally, more hairpins and targets in multiple detection platform may slightly affect the target detection. Although the LODs of multiple detection platform were inferior to the LODs of single miRNA detection platform, the multiplex platform with high efficiency can circumvent false positive/negative results. The performance was comparable to other previously reported multiplex probes, but inferior to DNAzyme/fluorescence method (Table S3). However, it is worth noting that the multiple miRNAs strategy without enzyme was beneficial for analyzing miRNAs in an extremely complicated sample matrix.
## 3.6. Ultrasensitive simultaneously detecting AMI-related miRNAs in human serums
Considering the significance of multiple AMI-related miRNAs anal­ ysis in complicated biological samples, the strategy for three AMIrelated miRNAs in human serums was explored. The results of human serum sample analysis are illustrated in Table S4. Three AMI-related miRNAs could be amplified with obvious fluorescence enhanced signal simultaneously in a concentration dependent manner. Additionally, these data manifested that the relative standard deviations (RSDs) $(1.0{-}9.6\%)$ and recovery $(92\mathrm{-}109\%)$ ) were applicable to the serum samples in clinical diagnosis. Consequently, these results revealed that simultaneous determination of three AMI-related miRNAs in a single system was successfully accomplished.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, the developed work reported a novel, amplified and nonenzymatic multiplex method for analyzing AMI-related miRNAs by coupling Fe-MIL-88 and signal-amplified CHA technology with several advantages. First, multiple AMI-related miRNAs simultaneous detection could avoid false positive or negative results. Second, ultrasensitive miRNAs detection was realized owing to the circular generation of threebranched fluorescence signal probes. Third, the isothermal reaction was suitable for developing point-of-care and field-deployable minimized devices. Moreover, universal platform can be fabricated easily just by changing the sequence of beacons. Broadly, we anticipate that this multifunctional research can be extended to developing a variety of intracellular multiplex detection systems as well as field-deployable and portable devices, which has great potential in pathological studies and clinical diagnosis.
